      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does pH matter in cleaning formulations?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "pH determines the dominant ionic species in a cleaning solution and dictates which soil chemistry the product can address. Acid cleaners use hydrogen ions (H+) to protonate and dissolve inorganic mineral deposits such as calcium carbonate scale and iron oxide rust. Alkaline cleaners use hydroxide ions (OH-) to saponify fats and hydrolyse peptide bonds in proteins. Without appropriate pH, neither mechanism is thermodynamically favoured, rendering the cleaning chemistry ineffective regardless of surfactant concentration." }
