      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can an acid cleaner be used on metal surfaces?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Acid cleaners can be used on many metal surfaces for specific tasks such as rust removal, descaling, or bright dipping, but the acid type, concentration, and contact time must be carefully controlled. Phosphoric acid is widely used for rust conversion on steel because it reacts with iron oxide to form a relatively stable iron phosphate layer rather than aggressively etching the base metal. Hydrochloric acid is effective but attacks both rust and the underlying metal rapidly, requiring inhibitors and very controlled application. Certain metals — including aluminium, zinc, and galvanised steel — are also attacked by strong alkalis, making acid cleaners the preferred option for those substrates." }
