    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does pH affect the performance of anionic surfactants in cleaning formulations?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Anionic surfactants such as linear alkylbenzene sulfonates (LAS) and fatty alcohol ether sulphates (SLES) are most stable and most effective between pH 7 and 10. Below pH 4, the sulfonate and sulphate head groups may undergo acid hydrolysis, breaking the ester linkage and generating inactive fatty alcohol and sulphuric acid — this is particularly true for sulphate esters such as SLES. Additionally, at low pH, hardness ions (Ca2+ and Mg2+) in the presence of anionic surfactants can form insoluble calcium soaps, which consume active surfactant and generate a precipitate. Formulating anionic-based systems in acid cleaners therefore requires either chelating agents to sequester hardness, or the substitution of anionic surfactants with pH-stable alternatives such as amphoteric surfactants or alkyl polyglucosides." }
    },
