      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the mechanism by which alkaline cleaners remove proteinaceous soils?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Protein molecules are polymers of amino acids linked by peptide bonds (–CO–NH–). Under alkaline conditions (pH 9 and above), hydroxide ions attack the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the peptide bond in a nucleophilic acyl substitution reaction, cleaving the chain into smaller amino acid fragments and peptide fragments that are water-soluble. The rate of hydrolysis increases significantly with temperature and hydroxide concentration: at pH 12 and 60°C, proteinaceous soils such as dried egg white, blood residue, and milk protein films are hydrolysed within minutes. This mechanism is fundamental to the performance of clean-in-place (CIP) systems in dairy processing, beverage manufacturing, and pharmaceutical production environments." }
    },
