      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq" style="margin: 3em 0 2em;">
        <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1.2em;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        {[
          ["Why does pH matter in cleaning formulations?", "pH determines the dominant ionic species in a cleaning solution and dictates which soil chemistry the product can address. Acid cleaners use hydrogen ions (H⁺) to protonate and dissolve inorganic mineral deposits such as calcium carbonate scale and iron oxide rust. Alkaline cleaners use hydroxide ions (OH⁻) to saponify fats and hydrolyse peptide bonds in proteins. Without appropriate pH, neither mechanism is thermodynamically favoured, rendering the cleaning chemistry ineffective regardless of surfactant concentration."],
          ["What is the typical pH range for an industrial degreaser?", "Industrial degreasers designed to remove mineral oils, synthetic cutting fluids, and machine greases typically operate in the pH range of 9 to 13. This alkaline window enables two concurrent mechanisms: saponification of any ester-based oils present (converting them to water-soluble soaps and glycerol) and emulsification of non-saponifiable hydrocarbons via nonionic and anionic surfactant systems. The precise pH is determined by the substrate sensitivity, with lower values preferred for aluminium and zinc alloys, which are vulnerable to corrosive attack at pH above 11."],
          ["Can an acid cleaner be used on metal surfaces?", "Acid cleaners can be used on many metal surfaces for specific tasks such as rust removal, descaling, or bright dipping, but the acid type, concentration, and contact time must be carefully controlled. Phosphoric acid is widely used for rust conversion on steel because it reacts with iron oxide to form a relatively stable iron phosphate layer rather than aggressively etching the base metal. Hydrochloric acid is effective but attacks both rust and the underlying metal rapidly, requiring inhibitors and very controlled application. Certain metals — including aluminium, zinc, and galvanised steel — are also attacked by strong alkalis, making acid cleaners the preferred option for those substrates."],
          ["Why are neutral pH cleaners used on natural stone and hardwood floors?", "Natural stone — particularly marble, travertine, limestone, and slate — is composed primarily of calcium carbonate, which is chemically attacked by any acidic solution, causing etching, dulling, and surface dissolution. Conversely, strongly alkaline cleaners can degrade sealants and accelerate the breakdown of organic binders in stone composites. Hardwood floors are similarly sensitive: strong alkalis strip natural resins and degrade polyurethane finishes, while acids can leach tannins and raise the wood grain. A neutral cleaner (pH 6.5–8.0) based on nonionic or mild amphoteric surfactants removes everyday soils through physical emulsification and wetting without triggering any chemical attack on the substrate."],
          ["How does pH affect the performance of anionic surfactants in cleaning formulations?", "Anionic surfactants such as linear alkylbenzene sulfonates (LAS) and fatty alcohol ether sulphates (SLES) are most stable and most effective between pH 7 and 10. Below pH 4, the sulfonate and sulphate head groups may undergo acid hydrolysis, breaking the ester linkage and generating inactive fatty alcohol and sulphuric acid — this is particularly true for sulphate esters such as SLES. Additionally, at low pH, hardness ions (Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺) in the presence of anionic surfactants can form insoluble calcium soaps, which consume active surfactant and generate a precipitate. Formulating anionic-based systems in acid cleaners therefore requires either chelating agents to sequester hardness, or the substitution of anionic surfactants with pH-stable alternatives such as amphoteric surfactants or alkyl polyglucosides."],
          ["What is the mechanism by which alkaline cleaners remove proteinaceous soils?", "Protein molecules are polymers of amino acids linked by peptide bonds (–CO–NH–). Under alkaline conditions (pH 9 and above), hydroxide ions attack the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the peptide bond in a nucleophilic acyl substitution reaction, cleaving the chain into smaller amino acid fragments and peptide fragments that are water-soluble. The rate of hydrolysis increases significantly with temperature and hydroxide concentration: at pH 12 and 60°C, proteinaceous soils such as dried egg white, blood residue, and milk protein films are hydrolysed within minutes. This mechanism is fundamental to the performance of clean-in-place (CIP) systems in dairy processing, beverage manufacturing, and pharmaceutical production environments."],
          ["Are there cleaning applications where pH must be precisely buffered?", "Yes. Several industrial and institutional cleaning applications demand precise pH buffering to protect sensitive substrates or meet regulatory standards. Optics and semiconductor cleaning — where silica surfaces are cleaned — require very narrow pH windows because both strong acids and strong alkalis etch silicon dioxide. Medical device reprocessing is governed by standards such as ISO 15883, which specify pH ranges for washer-disinfectors to ensure both soil removal and instrument compatibility. Food contact surface cleaning in regulated environments must comply with food safety legislation, requiring specific pH levels post-cleaning to avoid off-flavour migration or surface degradation. Buffer systems such as phosphate, citrate, or carbonate couples are incorporated into these formulations to maintain pH stability throughout the cleaning cycle."],
        ].map(([q, a]) => (
          <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 10px; background: #FFFFFF;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;">
              {q}
            </summary>
